{{$NEXT}}

  - commands() returns a command wherever Bash executes one. Commands
    in $(...), backticks and <(...) / >(...) are extracted from
    assignments (x=$(cmd), prefix assignments X=$(cmd) command, arrays,
    subscripts), export / declare / local / readonly / typeset / unset,
    [ ], [[ ]] and (( )), the command word ($(cmd) arg), redirect
    targets and herestrings (> $(cmd), < <(cmd), <<< "$(cmd)") and
    heredoc bodies with an unquoted delimiter - in addition to
    arguments, case and parameter expansions. The pipeline or list
    behind a heredoc operator (cat <<EOF | sh, cat <<EOF && cmd) is
    extracted with before_op / after_op set. findings() and the
    Security::Checker rules see all of these
  - before_op / after_op connect commands of the same level, and a
    redirect is recorded on the command it belongs to; neither ends up
    on a command substituted inside that command
    (echo $(id) > /etc/passwd: the redirect is on echo)
  - network_to_shell also fires when a substitution sits between the
    fetcher and the pipe (curl "http://x/$(hostname)" | sh,
    X=$(id) curl http://x | sh)
  - POD: new LIMITATIONS section listing the substitutions the 0.20.5
    grammar does not parse and the walker therefore cannot reach
  - Source tree-sitter cannot parse is reported instead of passing as
    harmless. findings() appends one `parse_error` finding per
    unparsable region (unterminated quote, `$(` without `)`, stray
    token, Bash the 0.20.5 grammar does not know such as `!(*.txt)`),
    with message, start_byte / end_byte, source and - where the parser
    had to make up a token - missing. Security::Checker->check_source
    appends the same as an issue with rule `ParseError`, severity
    `high`. Findings and issues from the parsable parts are unchanged
  - New method parse_errors($source) returning those regions on their
    own
  - Security::Checker->new accepts `parse_errors => 0` to switch the
    ParseError issue off (default: on)
  - POD: commands() documents that its result is best effort for
    unparsable source
  - Fix Bash.pm: build the vendored grammar at most once per process
    instead of once per instance, so every check_source call shares one
    compiled tree-sitter-bash.so instead of copying ~10 MB and running
    gcc each time. A build that vanishes (e.g. a forked child outliving
    its parent) is rebuilt; a failed build is retried in the same
    tempdir. Test: t/96_grammar_build_once.t
  - New rule: InsecureDownload (high/medium) — curl/wget/fetch with
    TLS verification disabled (-k, --insecure, --no-check-certificate,
    --no-cert-check) or plaintext http:// URLs. Skips URLs inside
    -H / --header / --data-urlencode argument values
  - New rule: NetworkListener (high/medium/low) — nc/ncat listeners,
    socat TCP-LISTEN, ssh -R reverse tunnels and -D SOCKS proxies,
    plus one-liner web servers (python -m http.server, php -S,
    ruby -run -e httpd, npx http-server). php -S escalates to high
    when bound to 0.0.0.0, downgrades to low when bound to localhost
  - New rule: ForkBomb (high) — self-recursive function with
    backgrounding: :(){ :|:& };: and named variants. Detected by
    inspecting each function_definition's body for self-recursion
    plus | or & operators
  - New rule: PrivilegeEscalation (high/medium) — sudo/doas/pkexec
    opening root shells (positional shell name or -i/-s/--login flag,
    or sudo su chain), su to a privileged user, chmod SetUID/SetGID
    (symbolic u+s/g+s/+s and numeric 4755/2755/6755 — leading octal
    digit inspected), systemd-run --user --scope
  - EnvDangerousVars: add BASH_FUNC_ prefix matching (BASH_FUNC_*
    exports), Git worktree vars (GIT_WORK_TREE, GIT_INDEX_FILE),
    runtime preload vars (PYTHONSTARTUP, NODE_OPTIONS, PERL5OPT,
    RUBYOPT)
  - SensitiveAccess: extend with 13 additional credential paths
    (.docker/config.json, .gnupg/private-keys, .git-credentials,
    .netrc, .pypirc, .npmrc, .cargo/credentials, gcloud / azure / gh
    configs, /proc/*/environ, /etc/gshadow, /etc/sudoers.d/)
  - UnquotedExpansion: include leading $ in captured var so the
    rule returns $bar instead of bar
  - Add `requires 'Encode';` (Bash.pm uses Encode::decode for UTF-8
    validation)
  - Sync docs/SECURITY.md and docs/RULES.md with current rule state
  - Tests: add 38 new subtests for InsecureDownload, NetworkListener,
    ForkBomb, PrivilegeEscalation in t/70_new_rules.t (now 133 tests)
  - Add full POD to all shipped modules (Text::Treesitter::Bash,
    Security::Checker, Security::Rule, all 6 Rule classes)
  - Add docs/SECURITY.md, docs/RULES.md, docs/CODE-ANALYSIS.md,
    docs/SECURITY-RESEARCH.md, docs/SIMILAR-PROJECTS.md,
    docs/ALIEN-TREE-SITTER-PROPOSAL.md
  - Add project-local Claude skill treesitter-bash-security
  - Fix Checker.pm: hoist `require Text::Treesitter::Bash` to top-level `use`
    (was a perl-core violation: lazy optimization via require)
  - Walker: add background operator `&` to `_operator_text`
  - Walker: extend `_is_argument_node` with array, subscript, regex,
    extglob_pattern, arithmetic_expansion, brace_expression
  - DangerousFlags: rewrite on top of a data table; was dead-code (the
    `%DANGEROUS_FLAGS` hash was defined but never read)
  - DangerousExpansion: use single quotes around `${var@P/Q}` message
    (avoids Perl `@P` array-interpolation warning)
  - MissingAbsolutePath: allow shell builtins (cd, echo, read, exit,
    return, pwd, set, unset, alias, export, ...) and add common package
    managers / build tools / monitors to the allowlist
  - New rule: IFSManipulation (high) — IFS= overrides and $IFS-bound
    expansions
  - New rule: DangerousExpansion (high) — ${!var}, ${var@P},
    ${var=value}, nested $(...) in ${...} (CVE-2026-29783 class)
  - New rule: ReverseShellSink (high) — nc -e, ncat -e, socat exec:,
    bash -i with /dev/tcp, ssh ProxyCommand, mkfifo
  - New rule: DangerousFilesystem (high/medium) — dd of=/dev/sdX,
    mkfs*, fdisk, parted, : > /etc/..., truncate -s 0 system paths,
    shred, mount / loop / crypto
  - SensitiveAccess: extend with 13 additional credential paths
    (.docker/config.json, .gnupg/private-keys, .git-credentials,
    .netrc, .pypirc, .npmrc, .cargo/credentials, gcloud / azure / gh
    configs, /proc/*/environ, /etc/gshadow, /etc/sudoers.d/)
  - Tests: add t/40_edge_cases.t, t/50_findings_extra.t,
    t/60_rule_edge_cases.t, t/70_new_rules.t
  - Static verification: all 12 modules and all 8 test files pass
    `perl -c` against stubbed Text::Treesitter dependency
  - Bump $VERSION to 0.003 across all 11 modules
  - Fix EnvDangerousVars and UnquotedExpansion: each looks at the text
    of its own command only. A variable name that is merely mentioned
    (echo ENV, grep IFS file, /etc/ENV.conf, inside a string, read as
    $LD_PRELOAD) no longer fires; EnvDangerousVars wants an assignment
    (NAME=value, export, env). UnquotedExpansion ignores quotes,
    escapes, comments, heredoc bodies, [[ ]] and assignment values
    (DIR=$HOME/bin), and no longer misses $x/y after a $'...' string.
    A command nested in $(...), backticks or <(...) is reported once,
    for itself, not again for the command around it
  - New helper Security::Rule->mask_source($source) for rules that
    match on the text of one command
  - Fix findings(): dynamic_shell reads short options as clusters, so
    bash -lc, sh -ec, perl -le, perl -0777e, python3 -Bc, ruby -ne and
    node -pe are code passed as a string like bash -c always was, and
    node --print and --eval=CODE count too. Quoted flags ("-c") are
    seen. Flags that take an argument (perl -Mstrict, python -Wignore)
    do not make a cluster look like a code flag
  - Fix findings(): shell_interpreter, dynamic_shell and network_to_shell
    go by the command that really runs. sudo and doas (with their
    options), env (options, NAME=value words, -S), exec, nohup, command,
    time, nice and xargs are looked through, so curl x | sudo bash,
    env bash -c id and xargs sh -c id are reported like the bare shell
  - Fix findings(): command words are compared after quote removal, so
    \bash, b"a"sh and 'ba'sh are bash
  - New finding type unresolved_command for a command word that is only
    an expansion ($SHELL -c id, ${cmd}, $(which sh)), so a caller can
    treat it as unknown instead of harmless

